<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.2.1: Multiplying Monomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01591</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.2.1: Multiplying Monomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4da44fb0-d44a-4deb-8f20-ca5749a0688c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para>Since monomials are algebraic expressions, we can use the properties of exponents to multiply monomials.</para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((3x^2)(-4x^3)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Use the Commutative Property to rearrange the terms.</para>
  <para>    \(3\cdot(−4)\cdot x^2\cdot x^3\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply.</para>
  <para>    \(−12x^5\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((\frac56x^3y)(12xy^2)\) </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Use the Commutative Property to rearrange the terms.</para>
  <para>    \(\frac56\cdot12\cdot x^3\cdot x\cdot y\cdot y^2\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply.</para>
  <para>    \(10x^4y^3\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<para>Multiplying a polynomial by a monomial is done by applying the Distributive Property of Multiplication over Addition.</para>
<para>Find the product of each expression. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(7x(3x+6)\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(21x^2+42x\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(7b^2(9b-4)\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(63b^3-28b^2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(2k(5k^2+3k-1)\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(10k^3+6k^2-2k\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(4r^2s(2r^2+8rs-12s^2)\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(8r^4s+32r^3s^2-48r^2s^3\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Building Character: Judgement</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/994b9e4ed8642288a26ffdb02908f21e6872b829"/></media></figure><div>
      <para class="os-raise-mb-0">A key factor in making sound judgements is not jumping to conclusions. Your judgment will never be perfect, but you can strive to be aware of bias and make corrections when necessary. </para>
      <para>Think about yourself. When you face a problem that requires judgment, how many of these things are true?</para>
      <list>
        <item>
          I independently ask several people for their judgment, and I consider their reasons. 
        </item>
        <item>
          I try to separate my hopes and fears from the facts and beliefs supporting the judgment. 
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
     </div>
  </div>
</div></section>
</content>
